From ad8ecdb147c95914f8f592dd87dcb6e3aa09ff1e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rob Currie <rob@example.invalid>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 14:24:40 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/8] perf: a read of a translation's std::atomic hands the
 interpreter to the thread it waits for

roottest/root/multicore/testSetAddressLoop runs ./testSetAddress thirty-three
times, and one run took 21.7 s of the harness's 300 s budget: nine workers under
one mutex between two busy waits. Every C++ thread is a Python thread of one
interpreter, so the thread spinning on `while (counter < kNThreads) {}` holds
the GIL while the worker whose counter it waits for needs that GIL to count -
CPython hands it over only at its switch interval, and 24 ms of file opening
became 1.3 s per thread. The reads a loop condition makes now give the
interpreter up with time.sleep(0), as a C++ spin gives up its core: one run is
0.9 s, the whole test 33.9 s instead of 300 s and a TIMEOUT (the reason was
"still running after 300 s").

That read is also the C++ meaning of `while (flag) {}`: std::atomic<bool>
converts to its value, which the plain atomic has no __bool__ for, so a spin on
one only ever ended because the translated barrier was a Python name rebound by
the waiting thread. The test for it spins on a flag another thread stores false.

The generic home for the yield is cppstd/threads.py's atomic, which pycxx-only
translations would then get too; it is outside this worker's scope, so the
ROOT dialect's runtime subclasses it here instead.

(cherry picked from commit 4ce84ff03179c3082b3f0eb939884bee588a3054)
